    "used_text": "Review the following design proposal for a compiler that schedules a complete language model across a CPU and a neural processing unit. Explain which decisions belong to the source program representation, which decisions belong to the target implementation, and how the search should compare their costs. The compiler represents computation as a graph of tasks with explicit iteration domains, data dependencies, storage locations, and execution resources. Each task may contain a data flow graph with arithmetic operations, loads, stores, and loop carried state.\n\nThe search should consider merging producer and consumer tasks when intermediate values can remain on the device, but it must preserve every observable result and the original numerical rounding boundaries. It should also consider splitting a graph that cannot be mapped efficiently. Splitting introduces additional communication and may require temporary storage, yet it can reduce routing pressure and improve the initiation interval. A useful cost model must include those additional loads and stores, launch latency, synchronization, and transfer costs rather than summing isolated arithmetic operations alone.\n\nTiling changes both the work assigned to a core and the shape of intermediate buffers. Replication divides independent output regions across several task instances. Physical placement chooses which columns and rows execute those instances. These decisions interact: a larger tile may require fewer instances, a smaller tile may permit wider parallel execution, and the best placement"
